==English==

===Etymology===
From {{inh|en|enm|ulage}}, from {{der|en|xno|ulliage}}, from {{m|fro|*ullier||to fill a partially empty cask}}, from {{der|en|fro|oel||bunghole", literally, "eye}}, from {{der|en|la|oculus||eye}}. See French {{m|fr|ouillage}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/ˈʌlɪdʒ/}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun|~}}

# In a wine bottle, the empty space between the cork and the top of the wine.
# In a cask or barrel, the empty space, occupied by air, that is created by not completely filling the cask or barrel, or through spillage.
#* {{quote-book|en|title=The Excise Officer's Manual and Improved Gauger|author=Joseph Bateman|year=1840|passage=The dry '''ullage''' will be obtained in the same manner, the dry inches being used instead of the wet.}}
# The topping-up of such a barrel with fresh wine.
# In an industrial setting, the empty space in a tank, such as for fuel.
#* {{quote-book|en|title=Marine Regulations|page=234|publisher=Esso Shipping Company|year=1950|passage=Upon completion of loading, all cargo tanks shall again be inspected and '''ullages''' carefully measured and recorded, with temperatures, in the Cargo Record Book, Port Log and Supplementary Loading and Discharging Report.}}

====Derived terms====
* [[ullage motor]]

===Verb===
{{en-verb}}

# To [[gauge]] the amount of empty space between the top of a cask and the level of liquid inside it.
